 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T12:59+08:00",
 "answer": "**Probably yes, with some precautions. I couldn't get a live reading for this evening, so check the current number before you go.**\n\n- The latest figure I found is from the Borneo Post. Bario and Kapit recorded \"Good\" air quality at 8am on Saturday (Oct 17). Kuching's API was 65 at that time. Readings of 51–100 are classed as \"Moderate\".\n- Conditions have been improving. Earlier this month Kuching's API reached an unhealthy 111, then fell to 85. That 111 is a bit above the 100 threshold, and outdoor activities are postponed under official procedures when the API goes above 100.\n\n**What this means for cycling**\n- **API 65 or below (Moderate):** Most healthy adults can ride. Easy to moderate rides are fine. Skip hard intervals, because you breathe much more air when you push hard.\n- **API 100 or above:** Skip the ride, or train indoors.\n- **If you have asthma, heart or lung conditions, or are pregnant:** Be more cautious. Keep the ride short and easy, or skip it when the API is Moderate.\n\n**Practical tips**\n- Check the live reading on the DOE's APIMS portal (apims.doe.gov.my) or the Sarawak NREB API report. Haze can change within a few hours, and evening readings sometimes rise as the air cools and settles.\n- Watch for a scratchy throat, coughing, eye irritation or chest tightness. If you get any of these, stop and head home.\n- A well-fitted N95 or KN95 mask helps if you ride, though it makes hard efforts more tiring. Cloth and surgical masks do little against haze particles.\n- Choose flatter, quieter routes away from heavy traffic, which adds to the pollution.",
